//? Title: BLAST-Style Seed-and-Extend Alignment: What the Heuristic Retains
//? Subtitle: Exact words, ungapped X-drop traces, missed alignments and repetitive-input work
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Find locally similar pieces of a query and a database sequence without scoring
every possible alignment. Exact shared words provide candidate locations, and
ungapped extension scores their flanks until the running score loses too much
ground. We inspect a seven-byte example, retain the original synthetic DNA
alignment, and test both sensitivity and work. Datamunge implements a simplified
BLAST-style heuristic: raw scores, exact byte words, no gaps, and one retained
segment per diagonal. Its seed and extension inspection APIs share the actual
search mechanics.
)

\toc

> The method

An exact length-w word at query position i and database position j fixes the
diagonal j-i. Aligned byte pairs on that diagonal score +a for a match and -b
for a mismatch, where a,b are positive. The seed contributes aw. To its right,
let R_t be the sum of the first t flank contributions and
\(B_t=\max(0,R_1,\ldots,R_t)\). Stop at the first t for which
\(B_t-R_t>X\), or at a sequence boundary. The left flank is treated
independently. Retain each direction's best prefix, keeping the shorter prefix
on equal gains.

$$
S_{\rm anchored}=aw+B_{\rm left}+B_{\rm right}.
$$
\alttext(The anchored alignment score is the seed reward plus the best inspected prefix gain on each side.)

For a fixed seed, the two choices are independent, so this sum is the best
seed-containing segment among the inspected prefixes. It need not be the best
segment beyond a stopped flank, or an optimum that has no exact seed. Search
retains scores at least min_score and keeps only the highest score on each
diagonal. Equal scores on a diagonal retain the first seed's segment, even if
another matching region is separate. Returned ties have deterministic coordinate
order.

This construction is inspired by BLAST's seed-and-extend approach. The original
BLAST work also considers score-based word neighbourhoods and statistical
significance. The API here compares bytes exactly, including case and embedded
NUL bytes. It adds no reverse-complement search, alphabet rules, gaps, bit scores
or E-values. X is in raw integer score units; it is not an NCBI command-line
bit-score parameter.

> Simple example: a loss can recover at equality

Use query AAACCAA and database AAATTAA, seeding on the initial AAA. With a=2,
b=1, the seed scores 6. The right-flank contributions are -1,-1,+2,+2;
their prefix scores are -1,-2,0,2. With X=1 the second mismatch stops extension.
With X=2 equality permits recovery, yielding the full seven-byte segment with
score 8. The following diagram uses the actual trace values.

```{cpp, file="figures/blast_xdrop.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto low=dm::algorithms::blast_extend_trace("AAACCAA","AAATTAA",{0,0},3,2,-1,1);
  const auto high=dm::algorithms::blast_extend_trace("AAACCAA","AAATTAA",{0,0},3,2,-1,2);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(55,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=4.1;style.height=1.7;
  diagram.node("seed",{0,0},"Exact seed\nAAA / AAA\nscore = 6",style)
      .node("first",{5.5,0},std::format("First mismatch\nC / T\nrun = {}, best = {}",high.steps[0].running_score,high.steps[0].best_score),style)
      .node("second",{11,0},std::format("Second mismatch\nC / T\nrun = {}, best = {}",high.steps[1].running_score,high.steps[1].best_score),style)
      .edge("seed","first").edge("first","second");
  style.fill={255,237,213};
  diagram.node("stop",{11,-3},std::format("X = 1: stop\nlength = {}\nscore = {}",low.hsp.length,low.hsp.score),style)
      .edge("second","stop","2 > 1");
  style.fill={220,252,231};
  diagram.node("recover",{16.5,0},std::format("X = 2: two matches\nrun = {}\nscore = {}",high.steps.back().running_score,high.hsp.score),style)
      .edge("second","recover","2 <= 2")
      .text({8.25,2},"Query AAACCAA; database AAATTAA; word size 3")
      .text({8.25,-4.6},"Extension stops only when the loss is strictly greater than X.");
  diagram.save_svg("build/blast_xdrop.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/blast_xdrop.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The orange branch stops after two inspected mismatches and returns the seed. The green branch crosses that loss and recovers at the next two matches.)
\alttext(A seed with score six extends through running scores minus one and minus two. At the second mismatch, X equals one stops with score six, while X equals two continues to running score two and total score eight.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::string query="AAACCAA",db="AAATTAA";
  std::print("| X | query position | database position | pair | delta | running | best | stopped |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (int x:{1,2}) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::blast_extend_trace(query,db,{0,0},3,2,-1,x);
    for (const auto& s:trace.steps)
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} / {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",x,s.query_position,
          s.db_position,query[s.query_position],db[s.db_position],s.delta,s.running_score,s.best_score,s.stopped);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| X | query position | database position | pair | delta | running | best | stopped |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 1 | 3 | 3 | C / T | -1 | -1 | 0 | false |
| 1 | 4 | 4 | C / T | -1 | -2 | 0 | true |
| 2 | 3 | 3 | C / T | -1 | -1 | 0 | false |
| 2 | 4 | 4 | C / T | -1 | -2 | 0 | false |
| 2 | 5 | 5 | A / A | 2 | 0 | 0 | false |
| 2 | 6 | 6 | A / A | 2 | 2 | 2 | false |
// result_end
\caption(Discarded negative tails remain visible in the trace, while HSP endpoints use the best prefix. Direction-local gains start at zero.)

> Original synthetic DNA example

The original query TTGGATCCAATT is compared with
ACGTACGTGGATCCAATTGGCCAAGGTT using w=4 and the default scores. This is an
illustrative synthetic sequence, not an observed biological dataset. The main
eleven-base match TGGATCCAATT scores 22. Two shorter four-base hits score 8.
Seed locations now come directly from the core index rather than a second
report-side word-matching loop.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/blast_words.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string query="TTGGATCCAATT",db="ACGTACGTGGATCCAATTGGCCAAGGTT";
  const auto seeds=dm::algorithms::blast_seeds(query,db,4);
  const auto hits=dm::algorithms::blast_search(query,db,4);
  std::vector<double> x,y;
  for (const auto& seed:seeds) {x.push_back(seed.db_start);y.push_back(seed.query_start);}
  std::print("Shared word pairs: {}.\n\n",seeds.size());
  std::print("| score | query range | database range | query segment | database segment |\n"
             "| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& h:hits)
    std::print("| {} | [{},{}) | [{},{}) | {} | {} |\n",h.score,h.query_start,h.query_start+h.length,
        h.db_start,h.db_start+h.length,query.substr(h.query_start,h.length),db.substr(h.db_start,h.length));
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(x,y,"seed starts",dm::RGB{100,116,139},5);
  if (!hits.empty()) {
    const auto& h=hits.front();
    plot.lines({double(h.db_start),double(h.db_start+h.length)},
               {double(h.query_start),double(h.query_start+h.length)},"top HSP",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2);
  }
  plot.title("Exact word starts and the best retained diagonal")
      .x_label("database byte position").y_label("query byte position")
      .x_limits(0,26).y_limits(0,16).size(900,480).save_svg("build/blast_words.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Shared word pairs: 10.

| score | query range | database range | query segment | database segment |
| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 22 | [1,12) | [7,18) | TGGATCCAATT | TGGATCCAATT |
| 8 | [0,4) | [16,20) | TTGG | TTGG |
| 8 | [6,10) | [20,24) | CCAA | CCAA |
\image(figures/blast_words.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Points mark starts of exact four-byte words. The red segment joins the best HSP's half-open endpoints; its extension reaches beyond its seed-start positions.)
\alttext(A run of exact-word points lies on database offset six relative to query coordinates. A red diagonal marks query one to twelve against database seven to eighteen; two isolated shared words occur elsewhere.)

> Checking sensitivity: an alignment without an exact word

ACACAC and AGAGAG share no two-byte word, so w=2 returns no HSP. Yet the
five-byte aligned pieces ACACA and AGAGA score \(2-1+2-1+2=4\).
For this small control, the canonical Smith–Waterman routine with gap penalty
-100 gives an ungapped optimum: the maximum possible match gain is only 12,
so any path containing such a gap cannot beat a positive ungapped segment.
That reference establishes a missed alignment for this example; it does not
make the heuristic exact on other inputs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/blast_sensitivity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string query="ACACAC",db="AGAGAG";
  const auto reference=dm::algorithms::smith_waterman(query,db,{2,-1,-100});
  if (reference.score!=4) return 1;
  std::vector<double> words,best,optimum;
  std::print("| word size | exact seeds | retained HSPs | best returned score | ungapped optimum |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int w=1;w<=6;++w) {
    const auto seeds=dm::algorithms::blast_seeds(query,db,w);
    const auto hits=dm::algorithms::blast_search(query,db,w,2,-1,5,1);
    const int score=hits.empty()?0:hits.front().score;
    words.push_back(w);best.push_back(score);optimum.push_back(reference.score);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",w,seeds.size(),hits.size(),score,reference.score);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(words,best,"best returned",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .lines(words,optimum,"ungapped optimum",dm::RGB{217,119,6},2)
      .title("Longer exact seeds miss this weak local alignment")
      .x_label("word size w").y_label("raw score").y_limits(0,6)
      .size(900,480).save_svg("build/blast_sensitivity.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| word size | exact seeds | retained HSPs | best returned score | ungapped optimum |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 9 | 5 | 4 | 4 |
| 2 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 4 |
| 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 4 |
| 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 4 |
| 5 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 4 |
| 6 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 4 |
\image(figures/blast_sensitivity.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The plotting convention uses zero when no HSP is returned. The reference stays at score four; only word size one exposes seeds here.)
\alttext(The best returned score is four at word size one and zero at sizes two through six. The independently computed ungapped optimum is four throughout.)

> Checking work: repeated words can dominate

Let both inputs be n copies of A, with fixed w and \(m=n-w+1\).
Every query word matches every database word, giving S=m² seeds. A seed at
starts i,j inspects \(m-1-|i-j|\) flank pairs. All scores are positive,
so no X-drop stops them. The total is

$$
E=\sum_{i,j=0}^{m-1}(m-1-|i-j|)
=\frac{m(m-1)(2m-1)}3.
$$
\alttext(Repetitive inputs create m squared exact seeds and m times m minus one times two m minus one over three extension pair inspections.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| input length n | emitted seeds | predicted seeds | inspected flank pairs | predicted pairs |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int n:{8,16,32,64}) {
    const std::string sequence(n,'A');
    const auto seeds=dm::algorithms::blast_seeds(sequence,sequence,4);
    std::uint64_t pairs=0;
    for (const auto& seed:seeds)
      pairs+=dm::algorithms::blast_extend_trace(sequence,sequence,seed,4).steps.size();
    const std::uint64_t m=n-3,predicted=m*(m-1)*(2*m-1)/3;
    if (seeds.size()!=m*m || pairs!=predicted) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,seeds.size(),m*m,pairs,predicted);
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| input length n | emitted seeds | predicted seeds | inspected flank pairs | predicted pairs |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 25 | 25 | 60 | 60 |
| 16 | 169 | 169 | 1300 | 1300 |
| 32 | 841 | 841 | 15428 | 15428 |
| 64 | 3721 | 3721 | 147620 | 147620 |
// result_end
\caption(The counts are logical pair inspections from the same extension loop as search. Trace allocation is not used to claim a runtime speed measurement.)

This implementation copies and hashes length-w string keys. Its expected cost
is \(O((Q+M)w+SL+H\log(H+1))\), where Q,M are input lengths, S is
seed count, L bounds extension length and H is retained diagonal count.
Index/output storage is \(O(Mw+H)\); seed or trace inspection adds storage
for its owned records. Hash collisions can worsen indexing. The repeated-word
control has quadratic seeds and cubic extension work, contradicting a blanket
claim that this particular implementation must beat exhaustive alignment.

> Conclusion

The original eleven-base match and its shorter hits are retained. Core seed
inspection replaces duplicated word enumeration, and a shared extension loop
makes strict X-drop decisions and discarded tails visible. Native tests compare
all small binary seed-containing segments with exhaustive enumeration, check
raw byte words, deterministic diagonal reduction, inclusive score thresholds,
invalid inputs and extreme integer arithmetic.

Exact seeding, early drops and one-result-per-diagonal reduction are explicit
limitations. The API now rejects invalid scoring and coordinates, computes scores
in int64, and rejects results that do not fit the existing integer HSP layout.
These controls establish the behavior of the simplified algorithm; they do not
establish biological sensitivity, statistical significance or a hardware speedup.

> References

- Altschul, S. F., Gish, W., Miller, W., Myers, E. W. and Lipman, D. J. (1990). *Basic local alignment search tool*. Journal of Molecular Biology, 215(3), 403–410. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-2836(05)80360-2. The exact-word, raw-score ungapped variant here is narrower than the original method.
